this is john collins  the world record holder for paper  aircraft distance  you might remember john from the micro  dock we made about him a while back  and today he's going to teach us how to  fold some world-class paper airplanes  so i named the world record plane after  my wife the name of the design is  suzanne  this is how you fold suzanne  you start with the short side of the  paper up you take the top of the page  and put it against the left side making  a big diagonal  you unfold that  and then you take the top of the page  and put it against the right side making  the other diagonal  [Music]  so now when you unfold that you've got  an x in the page now with the first step  after that is to take the right hand  edge from the crease corner down to the  other crease and put that against the  diagonal fold  you do the same thing for the left side  you can either unfold that  edge or leave it folded and move the  other edge over  then you make a fold straight down the  top of the plane gets folded down right  straight across the center of that x  you have to line up the top of the  diagonals with the bottom of the  diagonals  one long crease from the top layer to  the bottom layer because in the next  move you're going to use those diagonal  creases you're going to fold those just  following the existing creases  now if you're not going to use tape  you're allowed to use tape on the world  record plane if you're just going to  lock it together and have a little fun  with this design you take the tab that's  pointed down and fold it up over the two  corners you just moved to the center  then you fold the plane in half  [Music]  and then you make the wing fold  [Music]  fast paper airplane  yep you throw it like a football the  faster it spins the straighter it flies  and you can also demonstrate  centrifugal force and processional  forces with this aircraft  and another unique part of this aircraft  is that the center of gravity is not  actually on the aircraft every rotate  every object rotates around its center  of gravity the center of gravity on this  one is right there in thin air  roll over about a third  it's okay if it's a little bit more than  the third  fold the third in half  fold that in half again we're going to  start turning it  into a circle  unfold  last time we folded it in half  put one end inside the other and then  remake this crease  [Music]  and then just lock the back of it  together  with just a little tuck right there  [Music]  this was one of those lucky planes that  it came unchanged right out of my head  and when you see the folding technique  you're going to go what's going on in  that guy's head  we're going to start with the short side  up  and we're going to fold in half  and now we're going to find the halfway  point the midpoint on this long creased  edge here and making just a little pinch  now we're going to find the one quarter  point which is the midway point between  our half point  we're going to use our quarter point  we're going to make a crease that goes  from the quarter point up to this corner  right here now we're going to squash  this flap right here  and open it up  and press it flat  flip it over and fold that flap the  other direction now what we're going to  do is start to put these points into  these pockets take this point down and  line it up with the bottom of the pocket  i'm going to flip it over  and i'm going to make another crease  what we're going to do now is start to  narrow this even more so in order to  narrow it enough to go in there  i'm actually going to roll this up and  over  [Music]  i'm just going to narrow it so that it  fits  just inside this triangle  now you can press it all flat  a lot going on let's do the other side  we've been up here like this  we want to go down just before we get to  this point here  so that we're just inside there now  we're gonna lift and narrow  just to the inside  insert it into the pocket  and press it flat  so we were down like this and now we're  going up like that  [Music]  now we're going to do  a squash fold on the front part of the  plane  now we're gonna flip it over  and i'm gonna fold the nose back  just between these two shoulder points  right here  now we're going to fold it in half  now we just need to make wings and  winglets and we're just about there  [Music]  and now we're just going to make some  winglets  i start parallel with the main wing fold  i'll get these guys parallel with that  crease  we'll lift the winglets so that they're  vertical let me get the uh  the correct wing trim here  this is a superb stunt plane circles  left circles right does a loop uh it'll  even do two loops outside if you if you  trim it uh  the really shallow up elevator you can  get it to actually do two loops in one  flight one will be a giant loop and the  other one might be a little kick loop at  the bottom as it comes around to recover  from the first big loop you'll see what  i mean take it outside and have some fun  this is the boomerang one  [Music]  if you enjoyed this video and want to  learn more click the link to check out  john's great big story

